From 98378eb365fc55473c3f839c564845ff92a76ac1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 07:50:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoRenderers: what a Blu-ray draws, at a whole scale, keeps
 its pixels

The graphics plane of a Blu-ray (its menus, the graphics of BD-J) is
1920x1080, and at 3840x2160 it was scaled with linear filtering. At twice
the size that mixes each pixel with its neighbours, a quarter on each
side: a line one pixel wide keeps three quarters of its contrast and a
dot about half, so the thin text of a menu looked faint.

Where the scale is a whole number in both directions (1920x1080 to
3840x2160, or 1:1), what the disc draws is now scaled without mixing its
pixels: each one becomes a block of whole pixels, as the disc drew it.
Any other scale, and every other overlay (subtitles among them), is
filtered linearly, as before. The rule is in OverlayWholeScale.h, with a
host test.
